-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+The record store uses **whole-visit revisions** to handle concurrent edits. When a submission arrives, it names its base revision ID. The server checks whether a newer revision exists; if so, both the earlier and the new submission become competing revisions for a coordinator to resolve.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+The system preserves the submitted visit as a complete unit without attempting field-level merge logic. Each revision is independently submitted, stored verbatim, and authored. If two workers edit the same visit from the same base state, neither submission is lost — both appear as unresolved review work until a coordinator picks one, rejects one, or manually combines them.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Submission Envelope
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+When a client submits a visit edit, it provides:
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+- Visit ID (UUID, created by the tablet)
+- Base revision ID (the server revision the edit was made from)
+- Submitted fields (the complete visit record)
+- Author ID
+- Idempotency key (prevents accidental duplicates on retry)
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The server rejects a submission if the base revision ID no longer exists or if the plot reference is invalid. A valid submission becomes a new revision for that visit.
+
+## Current-State Response
+
+When a client or reviewer retrieves a visit, the response includes:
+
+- Current revision (the latest uncontested submission)
+- Whether unresolved review work exists for this visit
+- If unresolved: identifiers of the competing revisions awaiting resolution
+
+A visit with unresolved edits cannot be exported until resolved.
+
+## Resolution
+
+When a coordinator resolves competing revisions, they choose one of the following:
+
+1. Accept the newer revision, discard the other.
+2. Accept the older revision, discard the newer.
+3. Manually combine them into a new revision (which creates a new review work item if other unresolved submissions exist).
+
+The system records the coordinator's identity, the resolution decision, and the submission IDs involved. This history is preserved for audit and explains the final exported state.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visit as a unit, simpler merge logic, field relationships preserved as submitted. Trades higher review volume for guaranteed scientific integrity. |
+| Revision identification | Server-assigned revision IDs on submission | Client-supplied revision identifiers | Server ID ensures stability; base-revision-ID submission makes conflict detection explicit. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact format and indexing strategy for the revision history database.
+2. Revision pruning policy for very old submissions (retention vs. archival).
+3. Coordinator UI layout and sort order for the revision comparison workflow.
